# notification-digest retention hygiene, applied by systemd-tmpfiles-clean
# (daily by default). Two rules:
#  - archive: every digest's markdown lands here forever otherwise; a year
#    of retention keeps anything plausibly wanted while bounding growth.
#    'e' = clean an EXISTING dir by age, never create or chown it (the
#    role's own directory task owns creation and the container-UID chown).
#  - backups: daily state.db snapshots (digest-backup.service); 7 days'
#    worth is plenty for the "restore cursors/dedup after a bad crash" case
#    these exist for. 'd' also creates the dir on tmpfiles --create runs.
#
# The OFFSITE copies of those same snapshots are pruned by
# digest-state-backup itself, not from here. A rule for them was tried and
# rejected on 2026-08-25: systemd-tmpfiles refuses the path with "Detected
# unsafe path transition /srv/backup-share (owned by packer) ->
# .../myapps-digest (owned by root)", its guard against ownership changing
# mid-path. Chowning the directory to satisfy it would weaken that guard for
# a share; pruning in the script keeps the retention next to the code that
# writes the files, behind the mountpoint check that already stops it acting
# on a phantom local directory.
e {{ myapps_digest_archive_dir }} - - - 365d
d {{ myapps_digest_backup_dir }} 0750 root root 7d
